<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thalassocracy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as thalassocratic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A thalassocracy is a state whose power rests primarily on control of the sea, maritime trade routes and ports rather than on large land territories. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a> is a classic example, dominating the straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> from the seventh to the thirteenth centuries. Such a commercial thalassocracy required less bureaucratic complexity than a territorial agrarian empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thalassocracy.jpg" alt="Thalassocracy" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Talessman at English Wikipedia (Thomas A. Lessman); historicair 23:27, 8 August 2007 (UTC); derivative work by SelfieCity · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carthage_323_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
